          {
            "id": "s1:p1",
            "text": "{{Redirect|Bird's Eyes||Birdseye (disambiguation)}}"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p2",
            "text": "Gilia tricolor (bird's-eyes, bird's-eye gilia, tricolor gilia) is an annual flowering plant in the phlox family (Polemoniaceae)."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p3",
            "text": "Range and habitat\nIt is native to the Central Valley and foothills of the Sierra Nevada and Coast Ranges in California. Its native habitats include open, grassy plains and slopes below {{Convert|2000|ft|meters}}. However, there has been some sighting reported on iNaturalist in Western Washington and Southern Western Canada."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p4",
            "text": "Description"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p5",
            "text": "Growth pattern"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p6",
            "text": "{{Empty section|date=July 2014}}"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p7",
            "text": "Leaves and stems"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p8",
            "text": "{{Empty section|date=July 2014}}"
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p9",
            "text": "Inflorescence and fruit\nFlowers have 5 green sepals and 5 bell-shaped fused petals, which are blue-violet at the end, descending to purple spots over yellow throats, hence the three for \"tri\"."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p10",
            "text": "Human uses\nSeeds of this plant are sold online as a garden flower."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p11",
            "text": "Subspecies\nGilia tricolor ssp. diffusa (Congd.) Mason & A. Grant \nGilia tricolor ssp. tricolor Benth."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
